{
  "image_name": "ab709608ca4b87a963362ae0e888aa25.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes several strategies for improving electrochemical conversion of sulfur–chalcogen chemistry, likely by controlling how chalcogen species behave at different length scales and interfaces. Across the panels, the central theme is that carefully designed hosts, catalysts, membranes, and electrolytes can guide ion and electron transport, suppress unwanted side reactions, and speed desired redox steps.\n\nOne visible comparison shows a “micro” host versus a “nano” host, suggesting nanosizing increases accessible surface area and changes charge transfer around chalcogen particles. Another panel depicts a polar conductive honeycomb-like framework bearing surface groups, implying a host that both conducts and chemically binds reactive intermediates. A larger schematic combines solid catalysts with dissolved redox mediators to convert soluble polychalcogenide species, illustrating both heterogeneous and homogeneous catalytic pathways.\n\nOther panels emphasize atomic-scale tuning and interfacial control: substitutional cations in a chalcogen lattice appear to alter electronic states near the Fermi level, while catalyst-coated current collectors promote adsorption and faster reaction kinetics, reducing metal plating problems. The bottom row contrasts a conventional liquid electrolyte with a zwitterionic polymer electrolyte containing cationic and anionic groups, and shows a selective membrane that appears to regulate transport and block plated metal migration.",
